      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Supravalvular aortic stenosis is characterized by obstruction of the left ventricular outflow tract distal to the aortic valve, and may result in diminished coronary artery blood flow. This report describes the cases of 2 patients in whom obstruction to left coronary artery flow was caused by obliteration of the coronary ostium itself. This mechanism differs from the more commonly recognized cause--valve leaflet adhesion to the obstructing ridge of aortic tissue. The coronary artery obstruction found in these 2 patients required direct enlargement of the left coronary ostium in both. This mechanism of impaired coronary artery flow deserves emphasis, as traditional methods of extended patch aortoplasty may fail to relieve the coronary ostial narrowing."},
